"img": "cutouts/flicker.png", "w": 800, "h": 579, "len": 732.0, "beak": [0.66, 0.07, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "AA/AAAAP/AAAH/gAAD/AAAB/gAEA/4AHAf+ADgH/wDwD/4DwA/+B4Af/h4AH/x4AD/54AA//4AAf/4AAH98AAD79AAA+8AAAP/AAAH8gAAD+AAAA+AAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 10, "first": "9:25 AM", "last": "10:04 AM", "ts": 1793280351, "mins": [565, 566, 566, 566, 602, 602, 603, 603, 604, 604], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "White-throated Sparrow", "sci": "Zonotrichia albicollis", "n": 5, "first": "9:26 AM", "last": "10:00 AM", "ts": 1793280363, "mins": [566, 588, 591, 592, 600], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "White-throats come in two color forms, white-striped and tan-striped, and almost always pair with the opposite form. Listen for “Oh-sweet-Canada, Canada, Canada.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113071", "snd": "sounds/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113140", "sndby": "Matt Wistrand, xeno-canto XC339648, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "whitethroated", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/whitethroated.png", "w": 800, "h": 460, "len": 599.0, "beak": [0.73, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 18, "b": "AAB+AAAB/gAAB/8DAB/+BwB//h4B//54f//84P///8AAP/8AAA/8AAAD8AAAA/AAAA8AAAA/AAAAeAAAAeAAAAeAAAAHAAAA"}}, {"name": "Red-bellied Woodpecker", "sci": "Melanerpes carolinus", "n": 3, "first": "9:28 AM", "last": "9:46 AM", "ts": 1793280520, "mins": [568, 569, 586], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "The “red belly” is only a faint pink blush you will rarely see. The red cap is the giveaway. Its barbed tongue can reach about two inches past the bill tip.", "vox": "sounds/voice/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113012", "snd": "sounds/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113332", "sndby": "Bruce Lagerquist, xeno-canto XC713255, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redbellied", "cm": 24.0, "img": "cutouts/redbellied.png", "w": 400, "h": 800, "len": 516.0, "beak": [0.52, 0.23, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 64, "b": ""}}, {"name": "Pileated Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryocopus pileatus", "n": 2, "first": "9:33 AM", "last": "9:36 AM", "ts": 1793280786, "mins": [573, 576], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It chisels big rectangular holes in dead wood while hunting carpenter ants. Abandoned holes later shelter owls, ducks and other cavity nesters.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryocopus-pileatus.mp3?v=1793112997", "snd": "sounds/dryocopus-pileatus.mp3?v=1793113171", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC102028, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "pileated", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/pileated.png", "w": 649, "h": 800, "len": 737.0, "beak": [0.42, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 39, "b": "AAAf/gAAH/4AAB/+Bzwf/x/4H/9/8B///+Af/3/AH/8/8B//D/wf/wf+H/8D/x//Af+f/wP/n/8D/5//A////wH///8B////Af///wH///4A/9/+AP/f/gD/3/4Af5//AH+f/wA/v/4AP7//AB+//wAf//8AD///AAf//wAH//8ABv/+AAD//gAAf/4AAH//AAA//wAAP/8AAD/+"}}, {"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 5, "first": "9:40 AM", "last": "10:02 AM", "ts": 1793281253, "mins": [580, 587, 589, 590, 602], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 2, "first": "9:45 AM", "last": "9:51 AM", "ts": 1793281530, "mins": [585, 591], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//4AAP/+AAD//gAA//4AAP/8AAD//AAA="}}, {"name": "Ruby-crowned Kinglet", "sci": "Corthylio calendula", "n": 2, "first": "9:46 AM", "last": "9:46 AM", "ts": 1793281594, "mins": [586, 586], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its ruby crown stays hidden under olive feathers until the male gets excited, then it flares up. A ruby-crowned kinglet flicks its wings constantly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793112988", "snd": "sounds/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793113143", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC104679, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "rubykinglet", "cm": 10.8, "img": "cutouts/rubykinglet.png", "w": 800, "h": 653, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.09, 0.35, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "AAAAAwAAAAcAAAAGAAAADAAAADgAAAAwB8AAYA/wAOA//ADAH/8BwAf/hwAH/+YAB//8AAP//gAD//+AAf/jAAD/wAAAH4AAAD8AAAB+AAAA8AAAAfAAAA+AAAAeAAAAeAAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Tufted Titmouse", "sci": "Baeolophus bicolor", "n": 3, "first": "9:47 AM", "last": "9:59 AM", "ts": 1793281670, "mins": [587, 589, 599], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Titmice line their nests with hair, and they have been seen plucking it from live raccoons, dogs, and even people.", "vox": "sounds/voice/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793112971", "snd": "sounds/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793113083", "sndby": "Jim Berry, xeno-canto XC649331, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "titmouse", "cm": 16.5, "img": "cutouts/titmouse.png", "w": 574, "h": 800, "len": 653.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.25, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 45, "b": "cAAAAHAAAAAwAAAAIAAAAGgDAAB8B4AAPB8AAD5/AAA+/4AAeP+AAHv/wAB7/8AA+P/gAPhn4ACbH/AADz/wAA9/+AAPf/wABb/8AB+//gAO//4ABt//AAfP/wAHx/+AB+P/gAPh/8AHiP/AB47/wAeH/+AHofvgBvOB8AD3APgAfAB4APgAPAD4AB4A+AAfAPwADwA3AA8APwAHAD4AAAAfAAAAGwAAABgAAAAMAAAADAAA"}}, {"name": "House Sparrow", "sci": "Passer domesticus", "n": 1, "first": "9:59 AM", "last": "9:59 AM", "ts": 1793282347, "mins": [599], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Brought to Brooklyn from England in 1851, house sparrows spread across North America within decades. They are Old World sparrows, unrelated to our native ones.", "vox": "sounds/voice/passer-domesticus.mp3?v=1793113025", "snd": "sounds/passer-domesticus.mp3?v=1793113113", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC351547, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "life": true, "key": "housesparrow", "cm": 15.5, "img": "cutouts/housesparrow.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 672.0, "beak": [0.11, 0.11, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAA/wAAAf+AAAH/gAAAf8AAEH/gAHB/8ADgf/gHwH/8DwA//DwAP/54AD//4AAf/4AAD/8AAAf/gAAD/8AAD+PgAB8B8AB+APgB7AB4A8AAAA8AAAAOAAAAA="}}], "also": [], "date": "Thursday, October 29", "where": "", "updated": "10:07 AM", "sun": null, "code": "3bb51669b7", "listen": {"state": "on", "since": null, "control": false